---

<StandardPageLayout
  code='ASBE No. 15'
  standardType='ASBE'
  title={metadata.title}
  description={metadata.description}
  issued='2006-02'
  latestAmendment='2017-01'
  status='Superseded'
  summary={metadata.summary}
  bannerNotice={metadata.bannerNotice}
>
  <Fragment slot="toc">
    <PageToc
      items={[
        { id: "s1", label: "Conceptual Framework Linkage" },
        { id: "s2", label: "Scope â€” Long-Term Construction Contracts" },
        { id: "s3", label: "The Percentage-of-Completion Method" },
        { id: "s4", label: "Measuring Stage of Completion" },
        { id: "s5", label: "The Completed-Contract Fallback" },
        { id: "s6", label: "Immediate Recognition of Anticipated Losses" },
        { id: "s7", label: "How This Maps to the Current ASBE No. 14 Framework" },
        { id: "s8", label: "Worked Example â€” the Old Percentage-of-Completion Method" },
        { id: "s9", label: "Worked Example â€” Recognising an Anticipated Loss" },
        { id: "s10", label: "Disclosure Requirements (Historical)" },
        { id: "s11", label: "Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates" },
        { id: "s12", label: "Relationship to the Old IAS 11" },
        { id: "s13", label: "Statement Mapping" },
        { id: "s14", label: "Related Standards" },
        { id: "s15", label: "Glossary" },
      ]}
    />
  </Fragment>
  <div class="wrap">
<div class="callout warn" style="max-width:900px;margin:0 auto 20px;text-align:center;">SUPERSEDED STATUS: This Standard's substance was absorbed into ASBE No. 14 (Revenue) as part of the 2017 revenue standard revision â€” see the companion poster. This poster explains the historical framework, retained for reference and for understanding how long-term construction contracts are now analysed under the general revenue model.</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s1"><span class="num">1</span><h2>Conceptual Framework Linkage</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The predecessor to today's general revenue model</div><div class="principle">ASBE No. 15 GOVERNED the RECOGNITION of REVENUE and COSTS for LONG-TERM construction CONTRACTS, using a DEDICATED percentage-of-COMPLETION methodology â€” a SEPARATE, standalone FRAMEWORK distinct FROM ordinary sales REVENUE recognition. FOLLOWING the 2017 REVISION of ASBE No. 14 (see the COMPANION poster), this STANDARD'S substance has been ABSORBED into the GENERAL five-step revenue MODEL, PARALLELING how IFRS 15 ABSORBED the equivalent OLD IAS 11 internationally.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s2"><span class="num">2</span><h2>Scope â€” Long-Term Construction Contracts</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Contracts specifically negotiated for the construction of an asset</div><div class="principle">This Standard APPLIED to CONTRACTS specifically NEGOTIATED for the CONSTRUCTION of a SINGLE asset (or a CLOSELY interrelated GROUP of assets), TYPICALLY spanning MULTIPLE accounting PERIODS â€” BUILDINGS, infrastructure, LARGE industrial FACILITIES, and SIMILAR long-duration PROJECTS.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s3"><span class="num">3</span><h2>The Percentage-of-Completion Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Revenue and costs recognised by reference to progress</div><div class="principle">WHERE the OUTCOME of a CONSTRUCTION contract COULD be estimated RELIABLY, contract REVENUE and contract COSTS were RECOGNISED by REFERENCE to the STAGE of COMPLETION of the CONTRACT activity at the BALANCE Sheet date â€” the "PERCENTAGE-OF-COMPLETION" method â€” MATCHING revenue RECOGNITION to the PROGRESS of the WORK performed.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s4"><span class="num">4</span><h2>Measuring Stage of Completion</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Cost-to-cost, surveys, or physical proportion methods</div><div class="principle">STAGE of COMPLETION was MEASURED using WHATEVER method RELIABLY reflected the WORK performed â€” MOST commonly the PROPORTION of CONTRACT costs INCURRED to date RELATIVE to the ESTIMATED total CONTRACT costs (the "COST-to-cost" method), or ALTERNATIVELY surveys of WORK performed or COMPLETION of a PHYSICAL proportion of the CONTRACT work.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s5"><span class="num">5</span><h2>The Completed-Contract Fallback</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Where the outcome cannot be estimated reliably</div><div class="principle">WHERE the OUTCOME of a CONSTRUCTION contract COULD NOT be estimated RELIABLY, revenue was RECOGNISED ONLY to the EXTENT of CONTRACT costs INCURRED that were LIKELY to be RECOVERABLE, WITH contract COSTS expensed as INCURRED â€” a MORE CONSERVATIVE fallback APPROACH avoiding the RECOGNITION of PROFIT before OUTCOME certainty existed.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s6"><span class="num">6</span><h2>Immediate Recognition of Anticipated Losses</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">The full loss recognised as soon as it becomes probable</div><div class="principle">WHERE it BECAME PROBABLE that TOTAL contract COSTS would EXCEED total CONTRACT revenue, the ENTIRE expected LOSS was RECOGNISED IMMEDIATELY as an EXPENSE â€” REGARDLESS of the STAGE of completion REACHED, CONSISTENT with a PRUDENT, loss-RECOGNITION-forward APPROACH.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s7"><span class="num">7</span><h2>How This Maps to the Current ASBE No. 14 Framework</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Broadly the same economic outcome, differently derived</div><div class="principle">UNDER the CURRENT ASBE No. 14, a CONSTRUCTION contract MEETING the OVER-TIME recognition CRITERIA (customer CONTROLS the asset AS it's CREATED, or the ASSET has NO alternative USE with an ENFORCEABLE right to PAYMENT) PRODUCES a BROADLY SIMILAR economic OUTCOME to the OLD percentage-of-COMPLETION method â€” but the ANALYTICAL starting POINT is now the GENERAL five-step MODEL'S over-time CRITERIA, RATHER than a SEPARATE, construction-SPECIFIC methodology.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s8"><span class="num">8</span><h2>Worked Example â€” the Old Percentage-of-Completion Method</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cw span2"><div class="ctitle cw">Recognising revenue by reference to cost incurred</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A bridge construction project (historical treatment)</div><div class="wk-body">A construction company undertakes a bridge project with a total contract price of RMB 400,000,000 and estimated total costs of RMB 320,000,000. By year end, RMB 160,000,000 of costs have been incurred.</div><div class="wk-answer">Stage of COMPLETION = RMB 160,000,000 Ã· RMB 320,000,000 = 50%. Under the OLD percentage-of-COMPLETION method, the COMPANY recognised REVENUE of RMB 400,000,000 Ã— 50% = <strong>RMB 200,000,000</strong> for the year.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s9"><span class="num">9</span><h2>Worked Example â€” Recognising an Anticipated Loss</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">The full loss taken immediately, not spread over remaining progress</div>
<div class="worked" style="margin-bottom:0;"><div class="wk-title">Example: A contract turning loss-making (historical treatment)</div><div class="wk-body">Continuing the prior example, revised cost estimates now indicate total contract costs will reach RMB 420,000,000 â€” RMB 20,000,000 above the RMB 400,000,000 contract price.</div><div class="wk-answer">The company IMMEDIATELY recognised the FULL anticipated LOSS of <strong>RMB 20,000,000</strong> as an EXPENSE, REGARDLESS of the 50% STAGE of completion ALREADY reached â€” a PRUDENT, forward-LOOKING loss recognition RULE.</div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s10"><span class="num">10</span><h2>Disclosure Requirements (Historical)</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">What ASBE No. 15 previously required</div><div class="check-list"><span>Contract revenue recognised during the period</span><span>Method used to determine stage of completion</span><span>Amounts of advances received and retentions</span></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s11"><span class="num">11</span><h2>Critical Judgments &amp; Estimates</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2"><div class="ctitle cn">Where judgement mattered most</div>
<div class="two-col"><div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT JUDGEMENTS</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Whether the outcome of a contract could be estimated reliably</span></div></div>
<div><div class="elabel">SIGNIFICANT ESTIMATES</div><div class="dot-list"><span>Total estimated contract costs, underlying the stage-of-completion calculation</span></div></div></div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s12"><span class="num">12</span><h2>Relationship to the Old IAS 11</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card ci span2"><div class="ctitle ci">Comparison â€” the historical ASBE 15 vs the historical IAS 11</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Feature</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-warning);">ASBE 15 (historical)</span><span style="color:var(--color-text-info);">IAS 11 (historical, pre-IFRS 15)</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Percentage-of-completion approach</span><span>YES</span><span>YES â€” converged</span></div>
<div class="tr alt" style="grid-template-columns:1.2fr 1.2fr 1.2fr;"><span>Both standards' current status</span><span>SUPERSEDED, absorbed into ASBE No. 14</span><span>SUPERSEDED, absorbed into IFRS 15</span></div>
</div>
<div class="note">A GENUINELY parallel HISTORY â€” BOTH standards WERE broadly CONVERGED while ACTIVE, and BOTH were RETIRED at broadly the SAME point in the GLOBAL move toward a UNIFIED revenue MODEL.</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s13"><span class="num">13</span><h2>Statement Mapping</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cs span2"><div class="ctitle cs">Where every construction contract event landed (historical)</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Event</span><span>Statement(s) affected</span><span>Notes</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:1.6fr 1fr 1.4fr;"><span>Percentage-of-completion revenue</span><span><span class="badge bp">IS</span> <span class="badge bs">BS</span></span><span>Now governed by ASBE No. 14's general model</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s14"><span class="num">14</span><h2>Related Standards</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cd span2"><div class="ctitle cd">Cross-reference map</div>
<div class="td-table"><div class="th" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>Standard</span><span>Connection</span></div>
<div class="tr" style="grid-template-columns:0.8fr 2fr;"><span>ASBE No. 14</span><span>Revenue â€” the current, unified Standard whose 2017 revision absorbed this Standard's substance</span></div>
</div>
</div>
</div>

<div class="section-header" id="s15"><span class="num">15</span><h2>Quick-Reference Glossary</h2><span class="line"></span></div>
<div class="grid">
<div class="card cn span2">
<div class="glossary-item"><div class="glossary-term">Percentage-of-completion method</div><div class="glossary-def">The historical approach recognising construction contract revenue and costs by reference to the stage of completion reached at each reporting date.</div></div>
<div class="glossary-item" style="border-bottom:none;"><div class="glossary-term">Stage of completion</div><div class="glossary-def">The proportion of contract work performed to date, most commonly measured as costs incurred relative to total estimated contract costs.</div></div>
</div>
</div>
    <FinancialStatementKey />

  </div>
  <section id="history">
    <HistoryTimeline items={history.asbe15 || []} />
  </section>
</StandardPageLayout>



